<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Austria</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Austria is a country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a> with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a>. It was the hereditary base of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg dynasty</a>, which held the title of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-emperor">Holy Roman Emperor</a> for most of the period from the fifteenth century. Austria later formed the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire</a>, and it took part in the partitions of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polish-lithuanian-commonwealth">Polish-Lithuanian Commonwealth</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/austria.jpg" alt="Austria" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Bundesministerium für Landesverteidigung · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Austria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Austria is a landlocked country in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/central-europe">Central Europe</a>, lying in the Eastern Alps, and occupies 83,879 square kilometres. Only about a quarter of its area can be considered low-lying, and only 32% of the country lies below 500 metres. The Eastern Alps make up 62% of the total area, and the Alps of the west give way to lowlands and plains in the east. The Austrian granite plateau, part of the Bohemian Mass, accounts for 10%, and forest covers about 47% of the land. It is a federation of nine states, of which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> is the most populous.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>By the 6th century BCE the region was settled by Celtic tribes and served as the core of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hallstatt-culture">Hallstatt culture</a>; the city of Hallstatt has the oldest archaeological evidence of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/celts">Celts</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The Celtic Kingdom of Noricum was conquered by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a> in 16 BCE and lasted as a province until 476, while eastern parts fell in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pannonia">Pannonia</a> and the west in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carnuntum">Carnuntum</a>, an army camp turned capital, was home to 50,000 people for nearly 400 years. After the fall of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/odoacer">Odoacer</a> conquered most of the area in 487, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ostrogoths">Ostrogoths</a> followed by 493, and the Alemanni, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavarians">Baiuvarii</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avars">Avars</a> after them. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a> defeated the Avars in the late eighth century and set up frontier marches.</p><p>In 976 Emperor Otto II appointed Leopold I of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-babenberg">House of Babenberg</a> as margrave of the marchia Orientalis. The name Austria first appears in writing in 996, as Ostarrîchi. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/privilegium-minus">Privilegium Minus</a> made Austria a duchy in 1156, and the Babenbergs acquired <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/styria">Styria</a> in 1192; the line ended with Frederick II in 1246. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottokar-ii-of-bohemia">Ottokar II of Bohemia</a> then took control of the duchies until Rudolph I of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> defeated him at Dürnkrut in 1278. From then until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a>, Austria's history was largely that of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a>, and it became an archduchy in 1453.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Austria had established its own empire two years before the dissolution of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> in 1806, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austro-hungarian-empire">Austria-Hungary</a> followed in 1867. The empire's defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> led to the Republic of German-Austria in 1918 and the First Austrian Republic in 1919. An Austrofascist dictatorship under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/engelbert-dollfuss">Engelbert Dollfuss</a> formed in 1934, and Nazi Germany annexed the country a year before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-world-war">Second World War</a>.</p><p>After liberation in 1945 and a decade of Allied occupation, Austria regained its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> and declared perpetual neutrality in 1955. It joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> in 1995 and adopted the euro in 1999; its population was estimated at 9,220,882 in April 2026, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vienna">Vienna</a> exceeds 2 million.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Austria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Their speech is the ancestor of the Alemannic dialects of German, including Swabian, Alsatian and Swiss German, still spoken across south-western Germany, Alsace, Switzerland, Liechtenstein and Vorarlberg in <b>Austria</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/alemanni">Alemanni · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…the territorial upheavals of the Imperial Recess of 1803, so Francis took a new hereditary title, Emperor of <b>Austria</b>, as Francis I. The title gave a common name to the composite monarchy the Habsburgs had built since the sixteenth century: the Austrian hereditary lands, the Lands of the…</blockquote><a href="/empire/austrian-empire">Austrian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Habsburg dynasty had governed <b>Austria</b> and its associated lands for centuries, but the defeats of the 1850s and 1860s forced a reconstruction of the imperial structure: the loss of Lombardy in the Second Italian War of Independence (1859), and the Austro-Prussian War of 1866, which excluded Austria…</blockquote><a href="/empire/austro-hungarian-empire">Austro-Hungarian Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Tens of thousands of graves excavated in Hungary, Slovakia, <b>Austria</b> and Serbia document the culture of this period, especially the cast bronze belt fittings decorated with griffins and tendril patterns that distinguish the Late Avar era.</blockquote><a href="/empire/avar-khaganate">Avar Khaganate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Austria</b> began as an eastern frontier march of Bavaria and only became a separate duchy in 1156; Bavarian dialects are still spoken across most of Austria.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-bavaria">Duchy of Bavaria · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>The Peace of Münster in 1648 confirmed the division: the north, around 's-Hertogenbosch and Breda, became States Brabant, a territory ruled directly by the States General without a voice of its own, while the south remained under Spain and from 1714 under <b>Austria</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brabant">Duchy of Brabant · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
